IMPORT `fraud-s15`
IMPORT `fraud-penalty`

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35), sections 1 to 5: FRAUD.
--
-- Section 1 makes a person guilty of fraud if in breach of section 2 (false representation),
-- section 3 (failing to disclose information) or section 4 (abuse of position).  Section 5 says
-- what "gain" and "loss" mean in all three.
--
-- Every rule here answers the question "are the elements of this provision made out on the facts
-- given?".  Whether a person is in the end GUILTY is for a court: the rules do not decide any
-- fact for the person.  In particular, "dishonestly" is not defined by the Act and is a fact the
-- person states (fork F1 in NOTES.md).
--
-- Every offence in these sections is complete on the INTENTION alone: no gain, no loss and no
-- person deceived is needed (finding 1 in NOTES.md).

§ `Fraud (sections 1 to 5)`

§§ `Section 1: fraud`

-- src:7 | 1 Fraud
-- src:8 | (1) A person is guilty of fraud if he is in breach of any of the sections listed in subsection (2) (which provide for different ways of committing the offence).
-- src:9 | (2) The sections are—
-- src:10 | (a) section 2 (fraud by false representation),
-- src:11 | (b) section 3 (fraud by failing to disclose information), and
-- src:12 | (c) section 4 (fraud by abuse of position).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 1(1) and (2) (src:8-12)
GIVEN `the false representation case` IS A `False representation case`
      `the non-disclosure case` IS A `Non-disclosure case`
      `the abuse of position case` IS AN `Abuse of position case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 1(1) and (2): a person is guilty of fraud` `the false representation case` `the non-disclosure case` `the abuse of position case` MEANS
        `section 2(1): a person is in breach of section 2` `the false representation case`
    OR  `section 3: a person is in breach of section 3` `the non-disclosure case`
    OR  `section 4(1): a person is in breach of section 4` `the abuse of position case`

-- src:13 | (3) A person who is guilty of fraud is liable—
-- src:14 | (a) on summary conviction, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding the general limit in a magistrates’ court or to a fine not exceeding the statutory maximum (or to both);
-- src:15 | (b) on conviction on indictment, to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 10 years or to a fine (or to both).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 1(3) (src:13-15)
GIVEN place IS A Place
      `the date the offence was committed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE Penalty
`section 1(3): the penalty for fraud` place `the date the offence was committed` MEANS
    `the penalty subsection: what may follow conviction` 10 place `the date the offence was committed`

-- Section 1(4) (Northern Ireland reads the limit as 6 months) is stated once, with its four twins,
-- in fraud-penalty.l4.

§§ `Section 2: fraud by false representation`

-- src:17 | 2 Fraud by false representation
-- src:18 | (1) A person is in breach of this section if he—
-- src:19 | (a) dishonestly makes a false representation, and
-- src:20 | (b) intends, by making the representation—
-- src:21 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:22 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(1) (src:18-22)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `False representation case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(1): a person is in breach of section 2` `the case` MEANS
        `section 2(1)(a): dishonestly makes a false representation` `the case`
    AND `sections 2(1)(b), 3(b) and 4(1)(c): intends a gain for himself or another, or loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` (`the case`'s `what the person intends by making it`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(1)(a) (src:19)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `False representation case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(1)(a): dishonestly makes a false representation` `the case` MEANS
        `the case`'s `acts dishonestly in making it`
    AND `section 2(1)(a), (5): a representation is made` (`the case`'s `the representation`)
    AND `section 2(2): a representation is false` (`the case`'s `the representation`)

-- src:23 | (2) A representation is false if—
-- src:24 | (a) it is untrue or misleading, and
-- src:25 | (b) the person making it knows that it is, or might be, untrue or misleading.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(2) (src:23-25)
GIVEN `the representation` IS A `Representation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(2): a representation is false` `the representation` MEANS
    -- (a) untrue or misleading, and (b) the person making it knows that it is, or might be,
    -- untrue or misleading.  Fork F7: (b) is satisfied by awareness that it MIGHT BE untrue or
    -- misleading; nothing more certain is needed.
        (   `the representation`'s `untrue`
         OR `the representation`'s `misleading`)
    AND `the representation`'s `the person making it knows that it is, or might be, untrue or misleading`

-- src:26 | (3) “Representation” means any representation as to fact or law, including a representation as to the state of mind of—
-- src:27 | (a) the person making the representation, or
-- src:28 | (b) any other person.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(3) (src:26-28)
GIVEN `what the representation is about` IS A `What a representation is about`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(3): what a representation is about is within the meaning of "representation"` `what the representation is about` MEANS
    -- "Representation" means any representation as to fact or law, including a representation as
    -- to the state of mind of (a) the person making it or (b) any other person.  Anything else (a
    -- bare promise or opinion, say) is outside the definition: the definition says "means".
    NOT (`what the representation is about` EQUALS `something other than fact, law or state of mind`)

-- src:29 | (4) A representation may be express or implied.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(4) (src:29)
GIVEN `the form of the representation` IS A `Form of a representation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(4): a representation may be express or implied` `the form of the representation` MEANS
    -- Either form counts, so the form never decides the question: TRUE for both.
        `the form of the representation` EQUALS `express`
    OR  `the form of the representation` EQUALS `implied`

-- src:30 | (5) For the purposes of this section a representation may be regarded as made if it (or anything implying it) is submitted in any form to any system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to communications (with or without human intervention).
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(5) (src:30)
GIVEN `who or what the representation was made to` IS A `Who or what a representation was made to`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(5): a representation may be regarded as made when submitted to a person or to a system or device` `who or what the representation was made to` MEANS
    -- Fork F8: "may be regarded as made" is read as making it count, whether or not a human
    -- receives it.  A representation never communicated to anyone or anything is not made.
        `who or what the representation was made to` EQUALS `a person`
    OR  `who or what the representation was made to` EQUALS `a system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to communications`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 2(1)(a), (3), (4), (5) (src:19) (src:26-30)
GIVEN `the representation` IS A `Representation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 2(1)(a), (5): a representation is made` `the representation` MEANS
        `section 2(3): what a representation is about is within the meaning of "representation"` (`the representation`'s `what it is about`)
    AND `section 2(4): a representation may be express or implied` (`the representation`'s `its form`)
    AND `section 2(5): a representation may be regarded as made when submitted to a person or to a system or device` (`the representation`'s `who or what it was made to`)

§§ `Section 3: fraud by failing to disclose information`

-- src:31 | 3 Fraud by failing to disclose information
-- src:32 | A person is in breach of this section if he—
-- src:33 | (a) dishonestly fails to disclose to another person information which he is under a legal duty to disclose, and
-- src:34 | (b) intends, by failing to disclose the information—
-- src:35 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:36 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 3 (src:32-36)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `Non-disclosure case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 3: a person is in breach of section 3` `the case` MEANS
        `section 3(a): dishonestly fails to disclose to another person information which he is under a legal duty to disclose` `the case`
    AND `sections 2(1)(b), 3(b) and 4(1)(c): intends a gain for himself or another, or loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` (`the case`'s `what the person intends by failing to disclose it`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 3(a) (src:33)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `Non-disclosure case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 3(a): dishonestly fails to disclose to another person information which he is under a legal duty to disclose` `the case` MEANS
        `the case`'s `acts dishonestly in failing to disclose it`
    AND `the case`'s `fails to disclose information to another person`
    AND `the case`'s `the information is information which he is under a legal duty to disclose`

§§ `Section 4: fraud by abuse of position`

-- src:37 | 4 Fraud by abuse of position
-- src:38 | (1) A person is in breach of this section if he—
-- src:39 | (a) occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person,
-- src:40 | (b) dishonestly abuses that position, and
-- src:41 | (c) intends, by means of the abuse of that position—
-- src:42 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:43 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
-- src:44 | (2) A person may be regarded as having abused his position even though his conduct consisted of an omission rather than an act.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 4(1) (src:38-43)
GIVEN `the case` IS AN `Abuse of position case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(1): a person is in breach of section 4` `the case` MEANS
        `section 4(1)(a): occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person` `the case`
    AND `section 4(1)(b): dishonestly abuses that position` `the case`
    AND `sections 2(1)(b), 3(b) and 4(1)(c): intends a gain for himself or another, or loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` (`the case`'s `what the person intends by means of the abuse`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 4(1)(a) (src:39)
GIVEN `the case` IS AN `Abuse of position case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(1)(a): occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person` `the case` MEANS
    -- Fork F13: the Act does not say who must expect it, so the fact is taken as stated.
    `the case`'s `occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 4(1)(b), (2) (src:40) (src:44)
GIVEN `the case` IS AN `Abuse of position case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(1)(b): dishonestly abuses that position` `the case` MEANS
        `the case`'s `acts dishonestly in abusing it`
    AND `the case`'s `abuses that position`
    AND `section 4(2): conduct that is an act or an omission may be an abuse` (`the case`'s `the conduct that abuses it was`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 4(2) (src:44)
GIVEN `the form of the conduct` IS A `Form of conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 4(2): conduct that is an act or an omission may be an abuse` `the form of the conduct` MEANS
    -- Either an act or an omission may be an abuse, so the form never decides the question.
        `the form of the conduct` EQUALS `an act`
    OR  `the form of the conduct` EQUALS `an omission`

§§ `Section 5: "gain" and "loss"`

-- src:45 | 5 “Gain” and “loss”
-- src:46 | (1) The references to gain and loss in sections 2 to 4 are to be read in accordance with this section.
-- src:47 | (2) “Gain” and “loss”—
-- src:48 | (a) extend only to gain or loss in money or other property;
-- src:49 | (b) include any such gain or loss whether temporary or permanent;
-- src:50 | and “property” means any property whether real or personal (including things in action and other intangible property).
-- src:51 | (3) “Gain” includes a gain by keeping what one has, as well as a gain by getting what one does not have.
-- src:52 | (4) “Loss” includes a loss by not getting what one might get, as well as a loss by parting with what one has.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(2) (src:47-50)
GIVEN `the thing` IS A `Thing gained or lost`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5(2): the thing is property` `the thing` MEANS
    -- "Property" means any property whether real or personal (including things in action and
    -- other intangible property).
        `the thing` EQUALS `real property`
    OR  `the thing` EQUALS `personal property`
    OR  `the thing` EQUALS `a thing in action`
    OR  `the thing` EQUALS `other intangible property`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(2)(a) (src:48)
GIVEN `the thing` IS A `Thing gained or lost`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5(2)(a): gain and loss extend only to money or other property` `the thing` MEANS
        `the thing` EQUALS `money`
    OR  `section 5(2): the thing is property` `the thing`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(2)(b) (src:49)
GIVEN `how long it lasts` IS A `Permanence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5(2)(b): gain and loss include a temporary one and a permanent one` `how long it lasts` MEANS
    -- Either length of time counts, so it never decides the question.
        `how long it lasts` EQUALS `temporary`
    OR  `how long it lasts` EQUALS `permanent`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(3) (src:51)
GIVEN `how it is gained` IS A `Way of gaining`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5(3): a gain includes a gain by keeping what one has` `how it is gained` MEANS
        `how it is gained` EQUALS `by getting what one does not have`
    OR  `how it is gained` EQUALS `by keeping what one has`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(4) (src:52)
GIVEN `how it is lost` IS A `Way of losing`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5(4): a loss includes a loss by not getting what one might get` `how it is lost` MEANS
        `how it is lost` EQUALS `by parting with what one has`
    OR  `how it is lost` EQUALS `by not getting what one might get`

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(1), (2), (3) (src:46-51)
GIVEN `the gain` IS AN `Intended gain`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5: a gain within the meaning of section 5` `the gain` MEANS
        `section 5(2)(a): gain and loss extend only to money or other property` (`the gain`'s `what the gain is in`)
    AND `section 5(2)(b): gain and loss include a temporary one and a permanent one` (`the gain`'s `how long it lasts`)
    AND `section 5(3): a gain includes a gain by keeping what one has` (`the gain`'s `how it is gained`)

@ref Fraud Act 2006 s 5(1), (2), (4) (src:46-50) (src:52)
GIVEN `the loss` IS AN `Intended loss`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`section 5: a loss within the meaning of section 5` `the loss` MEANS
        `section 5(2)(a): gain and loss extend only to money or other property` (`the loss`'s `what the loss is in`)
    AND `section 5(2)(b): gain and loss include a temporary one and a permanent one` (`the loss`'s `how long it lasts`)
    AND `section 5(4): a loss includes a loss by not getting what one might get` (`the loss`'s `how it is lost`)

§§ `The intention common to sections 2(1)(b), 3(b) and 4(1)(c)`

-- src:20 | (b) intends, by making the representation—
-- src:21 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:22 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
-- src:34 | (b) intends, by failing to disclose the information—
-- src:35 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:36 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
-- src:41 | (c) intends, by means of the abuse of that position—
-- src:42 | (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or
-- src:43 | (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose another to a risk of loss.
@ref Fraud Act 2006 ss 2(1)(b), 3(b), 4(1)(c) (src:20-22) (src:34-36) (src:41-43)
GIVEN aim IS AN `Aim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sections 2(1)(b), 3(b) and 4(1)(c): intends a gain for himself or another, or loss to another, or to expose another to a risk of loss` aim MEANS
    -- (i) to make a gain for himself or another, or (ii) to cause loss to another or to expose
    -- another to a risk of loss.  The gain or the loss must be one within the meaning of section 5.
    -- The gain or loss need not actually occur: only the intention counts.
        (  CONSIDER aim's `a gain for himself or another`
           WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
           WHEN JUST gain THEN `section 5: a gain within the meaning of section 5` gain)
    OR  (  CONSIDER aim's `loss to another`
           WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
           WHEN JUST loss THEN `section 5: a loss within the meaning of section 5` loss)
    OR  (  CONSIDER aim's `exposing another to a risk of loss`
           WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
           WHEN JUST loss THEN `section 5: a loss within the meaning of section 5` loss)
